ommitted and will stick around for several years.\nAshley Montaine 9:04 A.M.: Brooke, Mr. Peters and I are both free Friday morning.\nBrooke Randolph 9:06 A.M.: Great. I’ll write an e-mail shortly."}],"question":"At 8:59 A.M., what does Ms. Randolph most likely mean when she writes, “Not at all\"?","bilingual":"Vào lúc 8:59 sáng, rất có thể cô Randolph có ý gì khi viết “Không hề”?","options":["She would like to participate in an interview.","She does not think Mr. Erickson should be hired.","She feels comfortable fulfilling a request.","She has not read Mr. Erickson’s writing."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Căn cứ vào nội dung bài đọc, đáp án chính xác là (C) \"She feels comfortable fulfilling a request.\".","vocabList":[{"word":"mean","pos":"Danh từ / Động từ (Noun / Verb)","level":"Level 3: 300 - 400 đ","ipa":"/min/","meaning":"điểm giữa; ở giữa; cách xa hai thái cực như nhau;"},{"word":"write","pos":"Động từ (Verb)","level":"Level 2: 200 - 300 đ","ipa":"/raɪt/","meaning":"viết, sáng tác"},{"word":"participate","pos":"Động từ (Verb)","level":"Level 3: 300 - 400 đ","ipa":"/pɑrˈtɪsəˌpeɪt/","meaning":"được tham gia; tham gia vào"},{"word":"interview","pos":"Danh từ / Động từ (Noun / Verb)","level":"Level 1: Dưới 200 đi","ipa":"/ˈɪn.t̬ɚ.vjuː/","meaning":"phỏng vấn"}]},{"questionNum":"174","passageTitle":"Questions 172-175 refer to the following online chat discussion.","passageText":"Ashley Montaine 8:54 A.M.: How did the interview with Mr. Erickson go?\nDan Campbell 8:55 A.M.: I really enjoyed meeting him. I think he’d be a great reporter\nhere. He seems smart and organized, and his samples show that he’s a great writer.\nAshley Montaine 8:57 A.M.: Brooke, can you contact Mr. Erickson to set up the next\ninterview? Is that a problem?\nDan Campbell 8:58 A.M.: I’d really like to work with him. It is very important that he\nimpress Mr. Peters.\nBrooke Randolph 8:59 A.M.: Not at all.\nAshley Montaine 9:00 A.M.: Thanks. I also see that he has a varied work history. That\nwill make him a well-rounded reporter.\nBrooke Randolph 9:02 A.M.: When would you like to meet with him again?\nDan Campbell 9:03 A.M.: Ashley, I believe you will participate in the next interview.\nNote that Mr. Peters is probably going to ask why Mr. Erickson wants to transition from\nfreelance writing to in-house news reporting. Also, Mr. Peters will want assurances that\nhe’s committed and will stick around for several years.\nAshley Montaine 9:04 A.M.: Brooke, Mr. Peters and I are both free Friday morning.\nBrooke Randolph 9:06 A.M.: Great. I’ll write an e-mail shortly.","passageTextVi":"","passages":[{"label":"Bài đọc","text":"Ashley Montaine 8:54 A.M.: How did the interview with Mr. Erickson go?\nDan Campbell 8:55 A.M.: I really enjoyed meeting him. I think he’d be a great reporter\nhere. He seems smart and organized, and his samples show that he’s a great writer.\nAshley Montaine 8:57 A.M.: Brooke, can you contact Mr. Erickson to set up the next\ninterview? Is that a problem?\nDan Campbell 8:58 A.M.: I’d really like to work with him. It is very important that he\nimpress Mr. Peters.\nBrooke Randolph 8:59 A.M.: Not at all.\nAshley Montaine 9:00 A.M.: Thanks. I also see that he has a varied work history. That\nwill make him a well-rounded reporter.\nBrooke Randolph 9:02 A.M.: When would you like to meet with him again?\nDan Campbell 9:03 A.M.: Ashley, I believe you will participate in the next interview.\nNote that Mr. Peters is probably going to ask why Mr. Erickson wants to transition from\nfreelance writing to in-house news reporting. Also, Mr. Peters will want assurances that\nhe’s committed and will stick around for several years.\nAshley Montaine 9:04 A.M.: Brooke, Mr. Peters and I are both free Friday morning.\nBrooke Randolph 9:06 A.M.: Great. I’ll write an e-mail shortly."}],"question":"What is indicated about Mr. Erickson?","bilingual":"Điều gì được chỉ ra về ông Erickson?","options":["He has never been on a job interview before.","He has held many different types of jobs.","He is taking over Mr. Peters’ position.","He is a former colleague of Ms. Montaine."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Căn cứ vào nội dung bài đọc, đáp án chính xác là (B) \"He has held many different types of jobs.\".","vocabList":[{"word":"interview","pos":"Danh từ / Động từ (Noun / Verb)","level":"Level 1: Dưới 200 đi","ipa":"/ˈɪn.t̬ɚ.vjuː/","meaning":"phỏng vấn"},{"word":"different","pos":"Tính từ (Adjective)","level":"Level 2: 200 - 300 đ","ipa":"/ˈdɪfərənt/","meaning":"không giống nhau; bất thường; đặc biệt"},{"word":"over","pos":"Danh từ (Noun)","level":"Level 2: 200 - 300 đ","ipa":"/ˈoʊvər/","meaning":"trên"},{"word":"position","pos":"Danh từ / Động từ (Noun / Verb)","level":"Level 1: Dưới 200 đi","ipa":"/pəˈzɪʃən/","meaning":"vị trí, chỗ (của một vật gì)"}]},{"questionNum":"175","passageTitle":"Questions 172-175 refer to the following online chat discussion.","passageText":"Ashley Montaine 8:54 A.M.: How did the interview with Mr. Erickson go?\nDan Campbell 8:55 A.M.: I really enjoyed meeting him. I think he’d be a great reporter\nhere. He seems smart and organized, and his samples show that he’s a great writer.\nAshley Montaine 8:57 A.M.: Brooke, can you contact Mr. Erickson to set up the next\ninterview? Is that a problem?\nDan Campbell 8:58 A.M.: I’d really like to work with him. It is very important that he\nimpress Mr. Peters.\nBrooke Randolph 8:59 A.M.: Not at all.\nAshley Montaine 9:00 A.M.: Thanks. I also see that he has a varied work history. That\nwill make him a well-rounded reporter.\nBrooke Randolph 9:02 A.M.: When would you like to meet with him again?\nDan Campbell 9:03 A.M.: Ashley, I believe you will participate in the next interview.\nNote that Mr. Peters is probably going to ask why Mr. Erickson wants to transition from\nfreelance writing to in-house news reporting. Also, Mr. Peters will want assurances that\nhe’s committed and will stick around for several years.\nAshley Montaine 9:04 A.M.: Brooke, Mr. Peters and I are both free Friday morning.\nBrooke Randolph 9:06 A.M.: Great. I’ll write an e-mail shortly.","passageTextVi":"","passages":[{"label":"Bài đọc","text":"Ashley Montaine 8:54 A.M.: How did the interview with Mr. Erickson go?\nDan Campbell 8:55 A.M.: I really enjoyed meeting him. I think he’d be a great reporter\nhere. He seems smart and organized, and his samples show that he’s a great writer.\nAshley Montaine 8:57 A.M.: Brooke, can you contact Mr. Erickson to set up the next\ninterview? Is that a problem?\nDan Campbell 8:58 A.M.: I’d really like to work with him. It is very important that he\nimpress Mr. Peters.\nBrooke Randolph 8:59 A.M.: Not at all.\nAshley Montaine 9:00 A.M.: Thanks. I also see that he has a varied work history. That\nwill make him a well-rounded reporter.\nBrooke Randolph 9:02 A.M.: When would you like to meet with him again?\nDan Campbell 9:03 A.M.: Ashley, I believe you will participate in the next interview.\nNote that Mr. Peters is probably going to ask why Mr. Erickson wants to transition from\nfreelance writing to in-house news reporting. Also, Mr. Peters will want assurances that\nhe’s committed and will stick around for several years.\nAshley Montaine 9:04 A.M.: Brooke, Mr. Peters and I are both free Friday morning.\nBrooke Randolph 9:06 A.M.: Great. I’ll write an e-mail shortly."}],"question":"According to the discussion, what is important to Mr. Peters about a new hire?","bilingual":"Theo cuộc thảo luận, điều gì là quan trọng đối với ông Peters về nhân viên mới?","options":["Prior news reporting experience","Ability to begin working immediately","Communicating well with colleagues","Staying with the company over the long term"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Căn cứ vào nội dung bài đọc, đáp án chính xác là (D) \"Staying with the company over the long term\".","vocabList":[{"word":"discussion","pos":"Danh từ (Noun)","level":"Level 1: Dưới 200 đi","ipa":"/dɪˈskʌʃ.ən/","meaning":"cuộc tranh luận, thảo luận"},{"word":"important","pos":"Tính từ (Adjective)","level":"Level 3: 300 - 400 đ","ipa":"/ˌɪmˈpɔrtənt/","meaning":"có ý nghĩa; có ảnh hưởng; thiết yếu; chủ yếu; phê bình"},{"word":"hire","pos":"Danh từ (Noun)","level":"Level 1: Dưới 200 đi","ipa":"/haɪr/","meaning":"sự thuê; thuê, mướn"},{"word":"prior","pos":"Tính từ (Adjective)","level":"Level 3: 300 - 400 đ","ipa":"/praɪər/","meaning":"sớm hơn; tiền lệ; trước"}]},{"questionNum":"176","passageTitle":"Questions 176-180 refer to the following article and letter.","passageText":"Alberta Business Matters\nApril issue\nImprove Your Office\nEnvironment Now!\nToday’s office environment, featuring\nnumerous corridors, unexciting beige or\nwhite walls, and often rows of identical,\nwindow less cubicles, might not inspire\ncomfort, beauty, and energy. However,\nthere are some easy, inexpensive ways to\nmake your office space more inviting.\nAir quality\n• Add some green plants to the decor.\nPlants offer a natural filtration system,\nincreasing oxygen levels.\nNonflowering plants should be\npreferred, as they will not scatter pollen.\n• A small, tabletop air purifier helps\nimprove stale air and removes dust.\nLight quality\n• Take breaks and go outdoors. Even just\nAlberta Business Matters\nfive minutes before or after lunch break\nwill provide your eyes with a respite from\nartificial light sources.\n• Use desktop lamps with full-spectrum\nlight bulbs.\n• Install double-glazed windows instead of\nblinds to reduce glare while maintaining\nnatural light.\nStress relief\n• Earplugs or noise-cancelling head phones\ncan block distracting noise in an open office\n\nfloor plan.\n• Photographs of loved ones and places we have\nvisited for vacation are reminders of our life\naway from the office. Select a few favorite\npictures as important decorative elements.\nDear readers, if you have tips to add to this\nlist, send them in and they will be published\nin next month’s issue.\nLetters to the Editor\nIt may interest your readers to know about the company I work for, called Move able, Inc.\nWe aspire to make dull offices more comfortable and convenient for workers, especially\nfor today’s on-the-move employees.\nFor example, say you work two days a week at your headquarters in Edmonton, and the\nrest of the week you are in a satellite office. Our “Can-Do Case” ensures that your favorite\noffice supplies always travel with you. Our “Modular Decor Kit,” weighing just 1.75 kg,\ncontains a portable reading lamp, a miniature silk plant, and a folding photo frame with\nspace for four pictures. Look us up online and follow us on social media, as we offer new\nitems frequently!\nBest,\nMaria Test a","passageTextVi":"","passages":[{"label":"Văn bản 1 (article)","text":"Alberta Business Matters\nApril issue\nImprove Your Office\nEnvironment Now!\nToday’s office environment, featuring\nnumerous corridors, unexciting beige or\nwhite walls, and often rows of identical,\nwindow less cubicles, might not inspire\ncomfort, beauty, and energy. However,\nthere are some easy, inexpensive ways to\nmake your office space more inviting.\nAir quality\n• Add some green plants to the decor.\nPlants offer a natural filtration system,\nincreasing oxygen levels.\nNonflowering plants should be\npreferred, as they will not scatter pollen.\n• A small, tabletop air purifier helps\nimprove stale air and removes dust.\nLight quality\n• Take breaks and go outdoors. Even just\nAlberta Business Matters\nfive minutes before or after lunch break\nwill provide your eyes with a respite from\nartificial light sources.\n• Use desktop lamps with full-spectrum\nlight bulbs.\n• Install double-glazed windows instead of\nblinds to reduce glare while maintaining\nnatural light.\nStress relief\n• Earplugs or noise-cancelling head phones\ncan block distracting noise in an open office"},{"label":"Văn bản 2 (letter)","text":"floor plan.\n• Photographs of loved ones and places we have\nvisited for vacation are reminders of our life\naway from the office. Select a few fav